Use case: stylized-concept. Asset type: production background illustration for an interactive fantasy game expedition map, landscape 16:9.
Input Image 1 is the approved game's forest key art: match its rich hand-painted fantasy storybook world, not its camera or characters. Image 2 is the approved Hazel character: match its finely painted angular brushwork, moss green/teal/aged-gold palette, detailed organic materials.
Create a beautiful BIRD'S-EYE / three-quarter overhead illustrated MAP of a single ancient forest kingdom, like a sumptuous hand-painted strategy game board. NO characters, NO horses. Not an eye-level landscape. The map fills the image without a frame. Deep pine and jade forests, small mossy limestone cliffs, a turquoise creek, ancient roots, tiny ruined arches, warm amber leaves, gentle localized golden light. Rich natural detail grouped into readable landforms, quiet open areas between landmarks. Dark deep teal forest at margins blending to #102722, no paper sheet, no UI panels.
Spatial layout is functional: seven SMALL distinct landmark clearings at normalized image coordinates from top-left, each landmark roughly 8% of image width, separated with ample open ground:
(12%,72%) a mossy forest entrance with two old stone gateposts;
(24%,40%) a small cluster of amber and red mushrooms;
(38%,64%) an oak bridge over a narrow turquoise creek;
(50%,30%) an enormous ancient banyan root arch;
(65%,53%) a courtyard of amber mineral stones;
(77%,28%) a sacred grove with a stag-antler-shaped carved stone shrine;
(88%,15%) a distant but legible small golden forest throne pavilion nested in tree crowns.
Only subtle NATURAL grassy passages meander through the forest between clearings. Do NOT paint graphic routes, thick roads, dotted lines, border lines, numbers, node circles, flags, labels or banners: those will be generated by game code later. Keep the central areas calm enough for small markers. NO text of any language, no letters, no watermarks. No vector triangles, low-poly trees, flat geometric clip art, photorealism or 3D render. Beautiful cohesive painted atlas, NOT an interface mockup.
